WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.340
Serde is a common and powerful way to deserialize structured data in Rust.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:13.560
It can turn JSON, YAML, TOML, or other formats into Rust structs and enums with relatively little code.

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.500
That is useful, but deserialization is not full validation.

4
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:29.120
A successfully deserialized value may have the right broad shape while still violating length limits, numeric ranges, cross-field relationships, authorization rules, or business invariants.

5
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.560
Required and optional fields should be chosen deliberately.

6
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:41.500
Optional fields can support compatibility and partial updates, but they can also hide missing data if the application silently applies defaults.

7
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.560
Default values are useful when they are part of an explicit contract.

8
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.620
They are dangerous when they cause incomplete or unexpected input to look intentional.

9
00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:59.640
Unknown fields should also be considered carefully because they may indicate client mistakes, version drift, or attempts to influence behavior.

10
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.840
Nested objects, arrays, and collections need validation beyond the top-level struct.

11
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:14.660
Arrays may be too large, contain duplicates, mix incompatible item types, or include items that are individually valid but invalid together.

12
00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.000
Nested values may need their own constructors or validators.

13
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:26.860
The application should avoid assuming that a parsed JSON object is safe merely because every field could be mapped into a Rust type.

14
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:38.820
Schema validation concepts can help teams document expected shapes and reject unexpected structures, especially when Rust services interact with other languages or public APIs.

15
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.880
Even then, domain validation still matters.

16
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:53.340
A good pattern is to deserialize into a transport type, validate and normalize that type, then construct domain types that represent what the application is actually willing to use.
